<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Security Engineering | Secure Lo-Fi Study Cafe</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">
</head>
<body class="admin-page">
  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="brand-lockup">
      <div class="coffee-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="steam steam-one"></span>
        <span class="steam steam-two"></span>
        <span class="steam steam-three"></span>
        <span class="coffee-cup">☕</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Security Engineering v3.1</p>
        <h1>Backend Security Architecture</h1>
        <p class="session-line">Node.js · Express · SQLite · Socket.IO · EJS</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="topbar-actions">
      <a class="button secondary small" href="/cafe">Back to Café</a>
      <% if (user && user.role === "admin") { %>
        <a class="button primary small" href="/admin">Admin Console</a>
      <% } %>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="admin-shell security-showcase">
    <section class="security-hero-grid">
      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Authentication</p>
        <h2>Server-side identity</h2>
        <p>Passwords are hashed with bcrypt using cost factor 12. Successful authentication regenerates the Express session before privileged state is attached.</p>
        <code>bcrypt · session.regenerate() · HttpOnly cookies</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Authorization</p>
        <h2>Role enforcement</h2>
        <p>Permanent-admin and moderator checks execute on protected server routes. UI visibility is not treated as authorization.</p>
        <code>requireLogin · requireModerator · requirePermanentAdmin</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Request integrity</p>
        <h2>CSRF and request correlation</h2>
        <p>State-changing HTTP operations require a session-bound CSRF token. Every request receives a UUID correlation identifier returned in the X-Request-ID response header.</p>
        <code>32-byte CSRF token · request UUID</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Abuse control</p>
        <h2>Rate limiting and validation</h2>
        <p>Authentication endpoints have a tighter request limit than general traffic. User input is length- and format-validated before database or media processing.</p>
        <code>express-rate-limit · express-validator</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Database</p>
        <h2>Parameterized SQLite</h2>
        <p>Application data and security telemetry are stored in SQLite. User-controlled values are passed through placeholders instead of SQL string concatenation.</p>
        <code>SELECT ... WHERE username = ?</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Output security</p>
        <h2>XSS-resistant rendering</h2>
        <p>EJS escapes normal server-rendered values. Live chat and avatar speech bubbles are inserted with textContent rather than trusted HTML.</p>
        <code>EJS escaping · textContent · CSP</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Uploads</p>
        <h2>Profile-image validation</h2>
        <p>Profile images are restricted to PNG, JPEG, and WebP, capped at 512 KB, and verified by binary file signature. SVG is rejected.</p>
        <code>MIME allowlist · magic-byte validation · size limit</code>
      </article>

      <article class="panel security-control-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Export safety</p>
        <h2>CSV injection defense</h2>
        <p>Admin audit exports neutralize spreadsheet formula prefixes before user-controlled values are written to CSV.</p>
        <code>= + - @ → prefixed before export</code>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">SQL Telemetry</p>
          <h2>Security event model</h2>
        </div>
        <span class="badge live">indexed</span>
      </div>

      <div class="security-schema">
        <code>event_uuid</code>
        <code>event_type</code>
        <code>severity</code>
        <code>actor_user_id</code>
        <code>username_snapshot</code>
        <code>target_user_id</code>
        <code>outcome</code>
        <code>http_method</code>
        <code>route</code>
        <code>request_id</code>
        <code>session_ref</code>
        <code>metadata</code>
        <code>created_at</code>
      </div>

      <p class="compact">
        Session correlation uses a keyed HMAC-derived reference. Raw session IDs, password hashes,
        CSRF tokens, and profile-image data are excluded from security-event records.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Detection Coverage</p>
          <h2>Events recorded in SQL</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="security-event-grid">
        <div><strong>Authentication</strong><span>registration, successful login, failed login, logout, rate-limit blocks</span></div>
        <div><strong>Authorization</strong><span>unauthenticated access, moderator denial, permanent-admin denial</span></div>
        <div><strong>Request integrity</strong><span>CSRF validation failure and correlated request ID</span></div>
        <div><strong>Realtime channel</strong><span>Socket.IO connect and disconnect with non-secret session correlation</span></div>
        <div><strong>Upload defense</strong><span>accepted profile updates, rejected type/signature/size, CSRF failure</span></div>
        <div><strong>Administrative audit</strong><span>moderation, deleted messages, music approvals, privilege changes, report exports</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Forensics</p>
          <h2>SQL investigation examples</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <pre class="security-code"><code>-- Failed login volume by username in the last 24 hours
SELECT
    COALESCE(username_snapshot, 'anonymous') AS attempted_user,
    COUNT(*) AS failure_count,
    MIN(created_at) AS first_failure,
    MAX(created_at) AS last_failure
FROM security_events
WHERE event_type = 'auth.login_failed'
  AND created_at >= datetime('now', '-24 hours')
GROUP BY COALESCE(username_snapshot, 'anonymous')
ORDER BY failure_count DESC;</code></pre>

      <pre class="security-code"><code>-- Follow one correlated session without storing its real token
SELECT created_at, event_type, severity, outcome, route, request_id
FROM security_events
WHERE session_ref = ?
ORDER BY created_at ASC;</code></pre>

      <p class="compact">
        Additional incident-response queries are stored in <code>sql/security-forensics.sql</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Trust Boundaries</p>
          <h2>Backend request path</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <pre class="security-code architecture-code"><code>Browser
  │
  ├── HTTPS / Express routes
  │     ├── rate limits
  │     ├── session authentication
  │     ├── CSRF validation
  │     ├── server-side RBAC
  │     └── parameterized SQL
  │
  └── Socket.IO
        ├── authenticated Express session
        ├── validated event payloads
        └── live presence / chat / player state

                    │
                    ▼
                 SQLite
        ┌───────────┼────────────┐
        │           │            │
   app records   audit_logs  security_events
                              + SQL indexes
                              + forensic queries</code></pre>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
